--TEST--
`Core\Json::decodeAs` reads a `Core\Time\Instant` field from the RFC 3339 text `Core\Json::encode` writes it as, and refuses every other spelling of the same moment — `rule:core-classes/derive-field-list`, `rule:core-classes/derive-generates-what-is-missing`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// JSON has no timestamp type, so `rule:core-classes/derive-field-list`'s `Core`
// value type crosses as the one text every reader of a document already agrees
// a moment looks like: RFC 3339, which is what `$at->toIso()` renders. The two
// `int` slots an `Instant` is stored in are this runtime's business and no part
// of what the document promises.
#[Core\Json\Derive]
final class Event {
    public string $name;
    public Core\Time\Instant $at;

    public function constructor(string $name, Core\Time\Instant $at) {
        $this->name = $name;
        $this->at = $at;
    }
}

var $booking = Core\Json::decodeAs<Event>("{\"name\":\"launch\",\"at\":\"2024-03-01T12:00:00Z\"}");
echo "name=", $booking->name, "\n";
echo "at=", $booking->at->toIso(), "\n";

// One spelling, read by both halves: what the encoder writes is the document
// the decoder was handed.
echo Core\Json::encode($booking), "\n";

// An offset is part of RFC 3339, so it is read rather than refused, and what
// it names is a moment and not a local reading of one: the field holds the
// same instant the line above decoded, which the rendering in UTC says.
var $offset = Core\Json::decodeAs<Event>("{\"name\":\"launch\",\"at\":\"2024-03-01T13:00:00+01:00\"}");
echo "offset=", $offset->at->toIso(), "\n";

// The bound on the other side. An epoch second is what a JSON *number* would
// carry, and a civil date is text that names no moment: both are reported
// against the field rather than guessed at, because a wire form that admitted
// either would make two documents mean one value.
try {
    Core\Json::decodeAs<Event>("{\"name\":\"launch\",\"at\":1709294400}");
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (ParseError $number) {
    echo "  ", $number->issues["0"]->path, ": ", $number->issues["0"]->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Core\Json::decodeAs<Event>("{\"name\":\"launch\",\"at\":\"2024-03-01\"}");
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (ParseError $civil) {
    echo "  ", $civil->issues["0"]->path, ": ", $civil->issues["0"]->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
name=launch
at=2024-03-01T12:00:00Z
{"name":"launch","at":"2024-03-01T12:00:00Z"}
offset=2024-03-01T12:00:00Z
  at: expected an instant as an RFC 3339 string, found a number
  at: expected an instant as an RFC 3339 string, found a string
